issue='The source asks for stronger lower bounds without fixing their asymptotic strength, the allowed degree as a function of the number of variables, a field, or a computational definition of the hard family’s explicitness. These choices determine a quantitative benchmark; they cannot be replaced silently by a chosen exponent or by the separate homogeneous-formula or unlayered-ABP questions.'
notes=['Recovered the general layered ABP model with affine labels and vertex count.','Separated the stronger-family question from the already tight bound for sum x_i^n and the unlayered edge-count question.','Preserved missing degree, field, explicitness and quantitative target; removed the suggested intermediate formula milestone.','Checked the later full version and 2026 multilinear literature, including the author’s notice that a claimed barrier has a proof gap.']
save(i,dict(title='Beyond quadratic lower bounds for general algebraic branching programs',formal='What asymptotically stronger lower bound than the source’s quadratic benchmark can be proved for explicit polynomial families against general layered algebraic branching programs (ABPs)? An ABP over a field F computes a polynomial in commuting variables x₁,…,x_n by summing, over all paths from its source to its sink, the product of the affine linear labels on that path. Its size is the number of vertices. The intended lower bound must hold for every such ABP computing the chosen family, with no homogeneity, multilinearity, variable-order or width restriction on the ABP. The source does not state the new rate, degree regime, field or uniform explicitness requirement, so this remains an incomplete quantitative question.',definitions='An ABP has finitely many layers V₀,…,V_ℓ, with V₀={s} and V_ℓ={t}, and edges directed only from V_j to V_{j+1}. Each edge e carries an affine form a_e,0+Σᵢa_e,i x_i with coefficients in F. A path weight is the formal polynomial product of its edge labels; the output is the sum of these products over all s-to-t paths, with equality as polynomials in F[x₁,…,x_n]. Labels may use all variables. Arbitrary field constants, repeated occurrences of variables and cancellation between paths are allowed. Define ABP_F(f) as the minimum Σ_j|V_j| over ABPs computing f. Counting edges or coefficient bits is a different measure. The polynomial’s total degree is the largest sum of exponents of a monomial with nonzero coefficient. An asymptotic family would consist of f_n in n variables, but both its degree bound and the algorithmic resource required to produce or access its coefficients still need to be specified. Merely choosing a polynomial of very large degree would introduce a depth lower bound unrelated to the intended improvement. The source’s example p_n=Σᵢx_i^n has ABP_F(p_n)=Θ(n²) when the field characteristic does not divide n, so a superquadratic bound cannot hold for this same family in this model.',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='A completed benchmark requires a fixed field convention, degree regime, explicit-family representation and resource bound, and a specified asymptotic lower-bound target. A result must then prove that every unrestricted layered ABP for the family has the required number of vertices. The present source passage does not determine those choices and therefore does not yet supply one complete acceptance predicate. A lower bound solely for homogeneous or multilinear ABPs, or solely for formulas, does not automatically establish the unrestricted claim.',context_blocks=[dict(title='What the quadratic result measures',text='The 2020 paper proves an Ω(n²) vertex lower bound for Σᵢx_i^n without assuming homogeneous ABPs. This matches a quadratic construction. It also treats degree-bounded polynomial edge labels, but the standard affine-label model is the baseline for this card.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A different question for unlayered graphs',text='The source separately asks for a nearly quadratic lower bound for unlayered ABPs computing the same power-sum polynomial. Unlayered programs and their edge measure are not the general layered-family improvement extracted here.',citation='journal'),dict(title='Why the quantitative choices matter',text='ABP variants simulate one another with polynomial overhead, but that overhead can erase a fixed polynomial improvement. This makes the vertex measure, affine labels and absence of homogeneity restrictions essential when comparing a proposed bound with the quadratic benchmark.',citation='journal'),dict(title='Later multilinear status',text='A January 2026 preprint reports that the best known lower bounds for multilinear ABPs remain quadratic and studies a limitation question for a particular lower-bound measure. This is adjacent evidence about a restricted model, not a stronger lower bound for the unrestricted ABPs of this card.',citation='multilinear2026'),dict(title='A claimed 2026 barrier has an acknowledged gap',text='A later preprint claimed an unconditional barrier for multilinear ABP lower bounds. Its 11 May 2026 revision notice reports a gap in Lemma 4.1 and says that all results depend on that lemma. The claim is therefore not recorded here as an established theorem.',citation='gap2026')],why='Stronger lower bounds for unrestricted ABPs would sharpen a central measure of algebraic computation between formulas and general circuits. The question concerns the cost of representing explicit polynomials despite sharing and cancellation.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='Quadratic vertex lower bounds for general layered ABPs are established for the power-sum family, matching its upper bound.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2022',text='The expanded full version retains the broad stronger-ABP question; its additional formula result does not specify the desired new ABP exponent or family.',citation='journal'),dict(date='2026-05-11',text='A multilinear-method barrier claim has an acknowledged proof gap in the author’s revision notice; it is not used as a verified result.',citation='gap2026')],status='uncertain',status_note='Read the conference definition and conclusion and the later author full version. Checked January 2026 multilinear status and the May revision notice for a claimed barrier; no matching unrestricted improvement was identified. The source still lacks a complete quantitative target.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(status='needs_specification',reviewed_on=DATE,notes=notes,remaining_issue=issue),review_note=issue,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A broad lower-bound question about unrestricted algebraic branching programs and succinct polynomial computation; existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Algebraic branching programs represent a polynomial as a sum of products along paths in a layered graph.','This question concerns the number of vertices when edge labels are arbitrary affine forms and no homogeneity restriction is imposed.','The source proves a tight quadratic bound for one power-sum family and asks for stronger lower bounds for explicit families.','It does not specify the desired rate, polynomial degrees, field or uniform construction requirement.','Those omissions remain visible, while separate multilinear results and a 2026 claim with an acknowledged gap are distinguished from progress on the general target.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','journal','multilinear2026','gap2026'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
